    <string name="illus_kidney_title">The kidneys and urinary tract</string>
    <string name="illus_kidney_caption">Each kidney receives blood from the aorta, filters it, and sends the clean blood back through the vena cava. The urine produced flows down the ureters to the bladder. When the kidneys fail, dialysis takes over part of this filtering.</string>
    <string name="illus_fistula_title">The arteriovenous fistula</string>
    <string name="illus_fistula_caption">A surgeon joins an artery to a vein (the anastomosis), usually at the wrist or elbow. The vein then receives a strong flow, widens and can be punctured with two needles: one takes blood to the machine, the other brings it back. The buzz you feel over it is the thrill.</string>
    <string name="illus_dialysis_title">How a dialysis session works</string>
    <string name="illus_dialysis_caption">A pump draws blood from the fistula into the dialyser (the artificial kidney). There, a thin membrane separates the blood from the dialysis fluid flowing the opposite way: waste, extra potassium and water pass into the fluid, and the cleaned blood returns to the body.</string>
